Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 03:19:26 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 108/119] scene-referred S8: the Nishita sky is physical after
 all, and 0.37 is derived

The note this replaces said the procedural sky's scale could not be reconciled
with the photometric one from source. It can. The model's phase functions are
normalised to mean 1 over the sphere and pair exactly with a 4*PI*K extinction,
so K is a scattering coefficient over 4*PI and the integral the engine evaluates
is the single-scattering integral with nothing missing. Its output therefore
carries whatever unit the sun intensity carries, and the renderer reads that
output as an engine radiance - so 1.0 of the dome is 10 000 cd/m2 like everything
else, and the "Sun intensity multiplier" is in units of 10 000 lux of
top-of-atmosphere illuminance. The shipped 50 asserts 500 000 lux against a
physical 133 000: 1.91 stops hot, and that one number is the whole discrepancy.
The scattering coefficients are 2.37x hot in all three channels at once, so the
dome's hue was always right and only its level was wrong.

r_SceneReferredNishitaScale therefore defaults to 0.37 = 12 000 / 32 000, and the
anchor is illuminance rather than zenith luminance because that is what
r_HDRDebug 1 measures, what lights the scene, what a probe bake integrates, and
what flattens the sun/shade ratio when it is too high. The residual - a zenith
about 1.9 stops under a bright-hazy sky - is the single-scattering model's own
error and is documented rather than tuned away, because the two anchors are 1.9
stops apart and one scalar cannot satisfy both.

The cvar also gains an on-change callback that forces a full dome recompute.
Without it the factor is consumed only when the time of day pushes a new
condition, and the dome is then rebuilt at 0.12 PER CENT of its texels per frame
- two texels, about 1024 frames, seventeen seconds - so typing a value at the
console would appear to do nothing and the calibration loop would be unusable.

And the sky pass gets a ceiling of its own, r_SceneReferredSkyPeakLuminance,
default 1.6e9 cd/m2: the physical solar disc, converted to engine units and
pre-exposed on the CPU, held under the writer clamp. The writer clamp is a fixed
number in EXPOSED units, so the real-world luminance it stands for moves with the
aperture - 1.1e9 cd/m2 at midday, 1.6e5 at night - which is right as an overflow
guard and wrong as physics. It bounds an approximation and says so: the dome has
no sun sprite, what reads as the sun is the Mie lobe, and capping that at disc
radiance still spreads disc luminance over degrees.

The calibration doc's section 2 is rewritten around the derivation, the
measurement that confirms it, the probe staleness the _rec stamp does not catch,
and the trap that a probe's Diffuse multiplier scales its BLEND WEIGHT, not its
brightness. ReC_Physical.env deliberately keeps the shipped multiplier of 50: the
preset-side equivalent is 18.5 with the cvar at 1.0, and doing both would be 1.4
stops dark.

research/s8-nishita-sky.md, decisions/s8-sky-and-probes.md part 1 approach A.

